#Disease Background
Spinocerebellar ataxia type 3 (SCA3), also called Machado-Joseph disease, is a progressive autosomal dominant 
neurodegenerative disorder and the most common inherited ataxia worldwide. It is caused by an expansion of a 
CAG trinucleotide repeat in the ATXN3 gene on chromosome 14, which translates into an abnormally long 
polyglutamine tract in the ataxin-3 protein. Normal alleles have about 12–44 CAG repeats, while pathogenic 
alleles expand to roughly 52–86 repeats, with longer repeats linked to earlier and more severe disease. 
The disorder primarily damages the cerebellum, brainstem, and spinal cord, leading to progressive loss of 
motor coordination. Clinically, patients present with ataxia (unsteady gait and limb movement), dysarthria 
(slurred speech), and abnormal eye movements such as nystagmus. Because SCA3 is autosomal dominant, a single 
expanded allele is enough to cause disease, and the condition shows genetic anticipation, where repeat length 
can increase across generations. Onset is typically in adulthood, with symptoms worsening progressively over time. 

#ATXN3 Gene and Normal Protein
The ATXN3 (ataxin 3) gene provides instructions for making the ataxin-3 protein, a deubiquitinating enzyme 
involved in cellular protein quality control. Normal ataxin-3 helps process and regulate ubiquitinated proteins, 
contributing to the maintenance of healthy protein function within cells. Mutations involving CAG trinucleotide 
repeat expansion in ATXN3 produce an expanded polyglutamine region in ataxin-3 and are associated with Spinocerebellar 
Ataxia Type 3 (SCA3).

